A||N||9||Bigfieroman||Fiero tire fitament, engine difficulties?||||5||
Z||000000||Bigfieroman||11-19-2000||03:27 PM||||I have a set of wheels, 16", with a 35mm offset.  Will 225/45-16s fit on the front? I think that 225/50-16s will fit on the rear...please help me out.  Also, my engine seriously lacks low rpm power! It's an 87' Gt automatic, 100k miles, and I have tried seafoam and some oil additives.  The engine really won't wake up until 3000 rpm.  I will push down, the engine will pull out slowly, accually bogging, and not really move me until 25 mph.  I have heard advice about removing the water seperator/air silencer fron just inside the side scoop for more air, but I wonder about it's effectiveness/safety for the engine?  Thanks a bunch for the help people, I really apprecciate it!<P>------------------<BR>I'm sorry officer, but it isn't my fault you can't catch me on the turns.  Want to go for a ride?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Philphine||11-19-2000||04:50 PM||||i have 16x7.5 with 35mm offset. i had 225/50-16s all around but have bigger wheels on back now. just got an identical set with 225/40-16s that came on them. no opinion on the size. haven't put them on yet.||||reg||1
Z||000002||2xsess||11-19-2000||06:07 PM||||as far as the lack of power, Sounds like a blocked up cat. to me.<P>Welcome to the forum. Where are you from?<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||11-19-2000||07:06 PM||||sorry. just reread post. i did'nt get bigger wheels, just put bigger tires on my wheels.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||11-19-2000||07:58 PM||||<B>Fitament?</B> Thats great, I love that word. I agress about a stopped up cat convert responsiable for the power loss. It might help to check the car in the dark, after running for a while. If it is your cat, the manifolds will glow redish orangeish. Also, might do a compression check. Check out the distributor and rotor also, could be either one.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"If you smoke after sex, you're doing it way to fast".</I>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bigfieroman||11-19-2000||08:34 PM||||Ahh, forgot to mention: soot comes from my tailpipes to the point that the back is always dirty after backing up.  Will the carbon ever end?  Should I be running premium?  I also still need info on that air dam removal.  Thanks everyone for the help.<P>------------------<BR>I'm sorry officer, but it isn't my fault you can't catch me on the turns.  Want to go for a ride?||||reg||4
Z||000006||Seanh||11-19-2000||09:31 PM||||Some one makes a decarbonizing kit. Its about $40. You should try that. I always run premium, and I think you should on all Fieros. Take a fairly light (thin) shop rag, and hold it over the tail pipe and have some one stomp the gas a couple of times. Check the rag and look for metal shavings, that could be a clogged cat.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tigger||11-19-2000||11:00 PM||||Hmm... can't be for certain, but the fronts sound like they might work.  On taking the air/water separator out... if you're real serious about getting a few more HP, then yes.  For everyday driving, possibly in the rain, I wouldn't.<P>BTW, I can get myself stuck in some pretty close fitaments too.  hoo hoo hoo hooo<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerwelcome.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||11-20-2000||05:08 PM||||I dont think you should remove the air/water seperator. It wont get you very much HP gain.||||reg||1
Z||000009||MikeE||11-20-2000||09:34 PM||||No offense to Seanh, but I disagree about running premium in all Fieros (unless you like to waste money).  Premium has no advantage in an engine without enough compression to require it in order to prevent preignition, and then the manufacturer will recommend it.  As far as I know, GM doesn't recommend premium for any Fiero engines, even the 3.4 DOHC in the Z34  didn't require premium (although I'm sure many owners use it anyway).  I believe many people use premium in cars that don't need it because they erroneously believe it will improve their performance or it somehow fools them and others into believing that their car is a higher performance car than it really is.  One so-called car expert stated in a Q & A column that running premium in a car that doesn't require it might make it run hotter but I don't see how it would since, theoretically, premium has less energy per volume than regular.<P>I have heard of cars that get so carboned up that they require premium to prevent knocking but that isn't the proper cure for exsessive carbon except in the short run.<P>That being said, everyone is entitled to waste their own money if they so desire. ||||reg||1
